In article <2782@tellab5.tellabs.com> seeya@tellabs.com (Mike Ciha) writes:
>In article <HAIRSTON.90Jun11144702@henry.ece.cmu.edu> hairston@henry.ece.cmu.edu (David Hairston) writes:
>This entire discussion about confirmations reminds me of a drawing 
>I have seen entitled 'Cowboy after OSHA'--It was a *very* humorous
>picture with every manner of safety mechanism imaginable to make the 
>cowboy/horse combination safer (i.e., a HUGE net surrounding the horse
>intended to catch cowboy if he falls, etc.)

By making the confirmations optional,  the nethack player is allowed to
select his own level of keyboard entry safety.  Those of us who want
"airbags and anti-lock" brakes can then choose to do so,  with no harm
to those who think they'll never get into an accident (or enjoy them when
they do.)

>The premise of the picture seems to apply here--it *IS* possible to make
>something so safe that nothing bad happens (unless the player confirms it
>first)

Do you think that the confirmation for attacking pets and peaceful monsters
should be eliminated?  After all,  you can tell a peaceful monster (most of
the time) simply by the fact that it doesn't move straight at you and
attack you.

>I REALLY have to agree with Dave's discusstion about falling into pools.
>I mean where will it all end??

With a keystroke programming language,  that lets you program things like

pickup:
If object "gray stone" then
  confirm
If object = "clear potion"
  If knownstatus(object,uncursed) then
    name(object,"water")
...

move:
If square(altar) then
   print "You move past a \align(square)\ altar"
...

>You totter on the edge of a trap filled with poison-tipped spikes..
>Fall in (y/n)?

This is different from confirmation of moving onto water.
When I play nethack,  the only time I would move onto water is if I have
water-travelling technology or teleportation ability,  or if I make a
keyboard entry mistake.  In the case of moving onto a square with a pit,
that is an unavoidable risk of moving around,  and I have no knowledge
that would tell me to avoid that pit.

(Mind you,  in the case of traps that have already been detected,  I would
like the option you propose above.  It's rather silly when you avoid the
teleportation trap or trapdoor you *wanted* to step on...)

>Yes, I agree whole-heartedly.  Careful play is what keeps you alive.

Characters shouldn't be killed by line noise or someone else causing
thrashing on a multi-user system,  however.

>A certain amount of randomness is what makes the game that much *more*
>interesting.

Fine,  let's modify the game so it throws in random commands into your
command stream?...  Dying by a keystroke accident is NOT interesting.
Infuriating?  Yes.  Aggravating?  Yes.  Provoking me to curse at Dave
Hairston?  Perhaps...:-)

>I suppose somebody at some point will request that a ^W (win game) option
>be put in........ :-)

Only as a non-scoring option.  :-)



